\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Graph energy and the constant modes}
\date{2026-10-16}

\p{Let #{R} be a commutative unital ring, #{V,E,s,h} finite incidence
data and #{w:E\to R} arbitrary weights. With the counting forms and
\vocabk{weighted graph Laplacian}{fdip-0007}
#{L=d_R^\dagger Wd_R}, one has
##{L^\dagger=L,\qquad
 b_V(f,Lg)=\sum_e w(e)(d_Rf)(e)(d_Rg)(e).}
The kernel of #{d_R} consists exactly of fields constant on each
connected component of the underlying undirected graph, and
#{\ker d_R\subseteq\ker L}. Two vertices are in the same component
when a finite path joins them, with edge orientation ignored.}

\proof{
\p{The weight map is self-adjoint. The adjoint laws give
#{L^\dagger=d_R^\dagger W^\dagger d_R=L}, and the defining identity gives
##{\begin{aligned}
b_V(f,Lg)&=b_E(d_Rf,Wd_Rg)\\
 &=\sum_e w(e)(d_Rf)(e)(d_Rg)(e).
\end{aligned}}
In particular, #{b_V(f,Lf)=\sum_e w(e)(d_Rf)(e)^2}. Each edge
occurs once and no factor #{1/2} is used. The equality #{d_Rf=0}
says that endpoint values agree along every edge, hence along every
path. Conversely, a componentwise constant field has zero difference
on each edge. This identifies #{\ker d_R} with the function module
on the components. Such a field also satisfies #{Lf=d_R^\dagger W0=0}.}
}

\p{The energy is unchanged by edge-orientation reversal: each reversed
edge negates both factors in its summand. Equivalently, the
[orientation-invariant return composite](fdip-0007) gives the same
Laplacian.}

\p{Perfection and unit weights do not give the reverse kernel inclusion.
Over #{\mathbb F_2}, take two vertices #{0,1} and two distinct edges,
both oriented #{0\to1}, both of weight #{1}. The counting pairings are
perfect and #{W=I} is invertible. Nevertheless,
##{\begin{aligned}
d_R(a,b)&=(b-a,b-a),\\
d_R^\dagger(u_1,u_2)&=(-u_1-u_2,u_1+u_2),\\
L(a,b)&=(0,0).
\end{aligned}}
For #{\delta_1=(0,1)}, one has #{d_R\delta_1=(1,1)\ne0}, but
its energy is #{1+1=0}. Hence #{\ker L=\mathbb F_2^2} strictly contains
#{\ker d_R=\{(a,a):a\in\mathbb F_2\}}. This failure comes from the
absence of real positivity, not from nonunit weights or nonperfect forms.}

\p{Now specialize to #{R=\mathbb R} and #{w(e)>0}. Write
#{D=d_{\mathbb R}}. The Laplacian is symmetric and positive
semidefinite, and
##{\ker L=\ker D,\qquad \dim\ker L=k,}
where #{k} is the number of connected components. The energy convention
and connected-graph argument appear in
\citet{Section 2.1, pp. 4-5}{spielman2009effective}; the ring identity
above and the disconnected, loop and parallel-edge extensions are
proved directly here.}

\proof{
\p{The energy identity specializes to
##{\langle f,Lf\rangle_V=\sum_e w(e)(Df)(e)^2\ge0.}
If #{Lf=0}, this finite sum of nonnegative real terms is zero.
Every summand is therefore zero; strict positivity of #{w(e)} gives
#{Df(e)=0}. The reverse inclusion was proved over #{R}. Indicators
of the nonempty components span the componentwise constant fields.
In a linear relation between them, evaluation at a vertex of any one
component makes its coefficient zero. Thus they are a basis, proving
the dimension statement. An action defined as half this
\vocabk{quadratic form}{ca-0004} uses a separate convention.}
}

\p{If #{V} is nonempty and connected, the kernel is the one-dimensional
space spanned by the constant field #{\mathbf1_V}. Its positive unit mode is
##{|V|^{-1/2}\mathbf1_V.}
An isolated vertex contributes
one component and one kernel basis vector. If #{V} is empty, the maps
#{s,h:E\to V} force #{E} to be empty too: both the component count and
kernel dimension are zero. Loops contribute zero energy, while distinct
parallel edges each contribute their own positive summand.}

\p{For the [alternating four-cycle](fdip-0003), put #{u=1+t}.
Direct multiplication gives
##{L_t=\begin{pmatrix}
u&-1&0&-t\\
-1&u&-t&0\\
0&-t&u&-1\\
-t&0&-1&u
\end{pmatrix}.}
For #{f=(a,b,c,d)}, the energy is
##{\begin{aligned}
f^TL_tf={}&(b-a)^2+t(c-b)^2\\
          &+(d-c)^2+t(a-d)^2.
\end{aligned}}
Every row of #{L_t} sums to zero, and for #{t>0} zero energy forces all
four values equal. The [comparison with the positive action](fdip-001R)
identifies how this edge energy combines with a mass term.}

\p{The hypotheses have concrete consequences. With vertices
#{0,1,2,3} and just the unit-weight edges #{0\to1} and #{2\to3}, the
matrix has two diagonal blocks #{\bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}1&-1\\-1&1\end{smallmatrix}\bigr)}.
Its kernel is #{\{(a,a,b,b):a,b\in\mathbb R\}}; connectivity is
needed for a single constant mode.}

\p{On two vertices with one edge #{0\to1} of weight zero, the underlying
graph is connected but #{L=0}, so its kernel is all of #{\mathbb R^2}.
For nonnegative weights the same proof identifies the kernel using only
the positive-weight edges. A zero weight also makes the proposed edge
pairing degenerate. If instead this edge has weight #{-1}, then
##{f^TLf=-(f(1)-f(0))^2,}
which is negative at #{f=(0,1)}. Positivity fails, and the edge form is
not an inner product. This example is negative semidefinite; two disjoint
edges with weights #{1} and #{-1} give an indefinite form, since fields
supported on the separate edges have energies of opposite signs.}

\p{For unweighted simple graphs, Mathlib's
\leanref{SimpleGraph.posSemidef_lapMatrix} proves positivity of its
Laplacian. That model has no separate weighted edge set; its energy
formula sums both adjacency directions and divides by two. The
edge-once sum above also handles loops and parallel edges, and is proved
directly here.}
